    "body": "### Problem\n\nRef: https://github.com/vim/vim/issues/20990\n\nThe current documentation :help diff_filler merely says:\n\n```diff_filler({lnum}) ... Returns the number of filler lines above line {lnum}. ...```\n\nHowever, filler lines can also occur after the last buffer line, at the end of the file. As these EOF filler lines are not above any actual buffer line, it is not clear from the documentation how the number of EOF filler lines can be obtained, or even how the EOF filler lines can be detected.\n\nA working solution (proposed by Gemini and perhaps informally known in the Vim/Neovim community) is to consider the EOF Filler lines to be above a virtual line `line(\"$\")+1`. However, only Vim/Neovim documentation can establish whether this is intended and supported behavior.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n:help diff_filler\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nDocumentation of diff_filler() should also state:\n\n***As a special case, if filler lines occur after the last buffer line, they are considered to be above a virtual line that follows the end of the file. Thus, if the buffer is not empty, diff_filler(line(\"$\")+1) returns the number of filler lines at the end of the file. If the buffer is empty, diff_filler(1) returns the number of filler lines.***\n\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-1281+g9f600c8914\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nYes, Vim 9.2.390\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nFedora 44\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nXTerm(406)\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nFrom release tar ball",
